; A match whose arms are all literals, with nothing to catch the
; rest. Exhaustiveness only ran when the scrutinee's type named a
; declared `data`, so this checked OK - and when no arm matched at
; run time, control fell through to the merge block and the match
; answered its freshly allocated result cell's contents: 0, at exit
; 0, indistinguishable from a real answer. docs/memory-model.md
; MM-VAL-9b; refused since 2026-08-14. (A bool match carrying BOTH
; `true` and `false` arms still checks - it cannot fall through -
; but only over a scrutinee that IS a `Bool`: over an `Int` those two
; are ordinary value tests covering 1 and 0, and `(match n ((true) 1)
; ((false) 2))` at `n = 7` answered the result cell. `armsBoolComplete`
; asked about the arms alone until 2026-08-17.)
;
; The guard also required that NO arm be a constructor, which quietly
; exempted every match whose arms are constructors but whose scrutinee
; type the checker cannot name - a lambda parameter, any result of a
; polymorphic function, a macro spine. Both the matrix check and this
; backstop were off at once for those, which is the only combination
; that is unsound; 557 is that case.
(:: main Int)
(fn (main) (match 7 ((1) 11) ((2) 22)))
